 "day_rule_in_words": "Per account that the reader gives a figure and with a non-OK day (IB-pr-464's set), take its rows inside the window in date order. A non-OK row (the reader's test, holdings_gold.py:246) is INTERIOR when it lies after the account's first OK row and before its last OK row (between two OK days, chained across); it is the LAST ROW when it is the account's final row in the window and non-OK. A non-OK row before the first OK row is a first-day exclusion and is not counted. A non-OK row after the last OK row that is not the final row (a trailing run longer than one) is neither of the two and is counted only in trailing_nonok_days_neither_interior_nor_last_row. days_counted = interior_days + last_row_days.",
 "prior_value_rule_in_words": "The producer's base is the row's OWN account_value: holdings_performance.py:856 sets account_value = included_value + basket_cash, from the PREVIOUS trading day's positions (holdings_return(prev_positions, day_positions, ...), :743-758; the comment at :857-858 'the whole t-1 book'), and the third np.select condition at :872 (account_value <= 0.0 -> NON-POSITIVE-BASE, assigned at :873-874) tests that same column; daily_return is account-value-divided at :876. The gold therefore carries the base in its own column, and the PRIMARY classes in this file use it: a day's 'prior value' is the row's own account_value, which is the account's basket at the close of the day before. The account_value on the account's PREVIOUS row is a different number, the basket at the close of the day before that, and so a proxy, not the base; it is reported separately for every cell under literal_previous_row (same tables, same sums). Not the reader's end_value: the reader reads the first and last OK rows' account_value (holdings_gold.py:274-275).",
 "primary_basis": "own_row_account_value",
 "zero_rule_tolerance": "Exactly 0.0 (v == 0.0). No tolerance. A nonzero value is negative or positive by its sign however small; the count of counted days within a cent (0 < |v| < 0.01) of zero but not zero is days_within_a_cent_of_zero_but_not_zero per cell under primary_own_row_base and literal_previous_row. no_prior_row: for the primary basis, the row's own account_value is null; for the literal rule, the account has no earlier row in the gold or it is null.",
 "zero_run_rule_in_words": "For a counted day whose prior value is zero, count the consecutive rows of the account in the gold (across all landed partitions up to 2026-10-08, not only the window) whose account_value is exactly 0.0, going back from the day's own row for the primary basis (row i is zero means the close of the day before is zero; 1 = only the close of the day before), and going back from the previous row for literal_previous_row (1 = only the previous row). Bins 1, 2, 3-5, 6-20, 21-60, more (over 60).",
 "primary_vs_literal_note": "The face asks for the prior row's account_value and also for the base column where the gold carries it; the gold does, so the primary tables use the row's own account_value. The literal previous-row reading is kept beside it in every cell (literal_previous_row) so the first can be read either way.",
 "control": {
  "what": "In memory only: an account valued positive, zero, positive over three days (5.0, 0.0, 7.0). Rule 'previous row' on three rows valued positive, zero, positive: the third day's prior value is read; rule 'own row' (the producer's base: the row's own account_value is the close of the day before) on the rows for the days after each close: the row that follows the zero close is read. A second plant, rows 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 4.0, reads the fourth day's prior value; a value of 0.004 is read by the exact-zero rule.",
